{
  "summary": "Adds wallet and RPC support for sending transactions to BIP 352 Silent Payment addresses. It introduces a PaymentDestination abstraction to accommodate dynamic, input-dependent destinations, adjusts coin selection to require derivation-eligible inputs whose private keys are accessible, derives taproot output scripts during transaction creation, and persists silent payment recipient metadata to allow RBF fee bumping.",
  "problem": "Bitcoin Core cannot fund, construct, or fee-bump transactions paying to BIP 352 silent payment addresses. Users wanting receiver privacy and address reuse prevention cannot pay silent payment recipients using Bitcoin Core wallet RPCs or the GUI.",
  "evidence": [
    "Tracked under the silent payments project tracking issue #28536 and serves as the second iteration of PR #28201.",
    "PR description highlights that 'recipient silent payments destinations are now persisted to the wallet to make it possible to RBF' and restricts unsafe operations such as creating PSBTs or sending unadded transactions with silent payment destinations."
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [
      "#35301"
    ],
    "enables": [
      "#32966"
    ]
  },
  "scope_notes": "Touches core wallet spending routines, coin selection, wallet database records, fee bumping, and RPC endpoints (send, sendtoaddress, sendmany, sendall, validateaddress). Also includes a standalone BIP 352 scanner in the Python functional test framework.",
  "changed_since_previous": "Replaced the node-level scantxforsilentpayments RPC with a BIP 352 scanning utility inside the Python test framework, refactored CreateRateBumpTransaction to return util::Expected, and made ConstructTransaction output parsing optional.",
  "card": "Adds wallet and RPC support for sending to BIP 352 Silent Payment addresses across send, sendtoaddress, sendmany, and sendall. Currently, Bitcoin Core wallet users cannot pay silent payment addresses, preventing the use of receiver privacy protocols in Core. The PR introduces PaymentDestination to handle dynamic output derivation, updates coin selection to ensure derivation-eligible keys are available, and stores recipient destinations in the wallet database to support RBF fee bumping. This work is tracked under project issue #28536, depends on prerequisite PR #35301, and helps unblock receiving support in PR #32966.",
  "needs": [],
  "confidence": "high",
  "uncertainties": [],
  "risk": {
    "level": "high",
    "touches": [
      "wallet",
      "wallet_storage",
      "rpc_api"
    ],
    "why": "Defects in output key derivation, input private key summation, or recipient persistence during fee bumps could create unspendable outputs or permanently burn user funds."
  }
}
